# Hyperactivity: A Complete First-Principles Exposition

This guide walks from zero to the live research frontier of **Hyperactivity** without hand-waving, hidden steps, or toy simplifications. Every construction presented here is a real, machine-checked object in the repository (`store/`), verifiable locally with `./hyperactive`.

Hyperactivity is not a conventional programming language, not a proof assistant bolted onto a 1970s file system, and not a statistical AI wrapper. It is a **cubical univalent interaction-net computing substrate** — a single mathematical architecture in which observation, identity, proof, parallel execution, native compilation, lossless compression, scientific modeling, and decentralized coordination are instances of the same few primitives.

---

## Orientation: Why Computing Broke in 1970

Almost every recurring failure in modern computing — broken syncing, API glue, platform lock-in, AI hallucination, irreproducible science, financial intermediation, and trillions of dollars spent recomputing the same work — traces back to three architectural choices made around 1970:

1. **Untyped byte files.** Storage was defined as raw bytes whose meaning belongs solely to the program that parses them. Data cannot cross program boundaries with its meaning intact.
2. **Location-addressed networks.** Because storage and compute were scarce in 1969, networks were built around *where* a resource lives (a server, URL, or account) rather than *what* it is. Whoever controls the location controls access, identity, and rent.
3. **No primitive notion of equality.** Computing never gave a mathematical account of when two objects are the same. Deduplication, caching, merging, versioning, schema migration, and chemical/scientific entity resolution all reinvent incompatible, lossy approximations of equality.

Hyperactivity starts over from the primitives that were skipped: **observation fibers**, **univalent equality**, **local interaction**, and **content-addressed exchange**.

