## Part I — Observation, Fibers, and Warrant

### 1.1 Partial Observation and Compatible Histories

No physical or computational participant ever observes the complete state of the universe. Let $H$ be the type of possible histories (or underlying states), $Y$ the type of observations (readings), and

$$o : H \to Y$$

the observation map sending each history $h : H$ to the reading $o(h)$ it produces.

When a participant receives a concrete reading $y : Y$, that reading does **not** select a single history $h$. It selects the **observation fiber** — the type of all histories compatible with $y$, paired with the witness that they produce $y$:

$$\mathrm{Fib}_o(y) \;=\; \sum_{h : H} \mathrm{Path}_Y(o(h),\, y)$$

In `lib/fiber.cub` and `lib/observation.cub`, this construction is proved equivalent to the whole space via the **fiber decomposition equivalence** (`fiberEquiv`):

$$H \;\simeq\; \sum_{y : Y} \mathrm{Fib}_o(y)$$

Every world state is canonically factored into *(what is observed, the compatible history inside its fiber)*.

### 1.2 Warrant: Why Rational Agents Cannot Hallucinate

Suppose a participant observes $y : Y$ and wants to act on a proposition $P : H \to \mathrm{Type}$ about the true underlying history. What does the reading $y$ actually **warrant**?

Not a guess at one plausible $h \in \mathrm{Fib}_o(y)$ — and not a statistical mode over $H$. A reading $y$ warrants $P$ if and only if the participant holds a **dependent construction over the entire fiber**:

$$\mathrm{Warrants}(o, y, P) \;=\; \prod_{w : \mathrm{Fib}_o(y)} P(\mathrm{fst}\, w)$$

Read this type carefully:
- If $P$ holds for **every** history compatible with what you observed, then acting on $P$ is provably safe — no unobserved detail can invalidate it.
- If there exist two histories $h_1, h_2 \in \mathrm{Fib}_o(y)$ such that $P(h_1)$ holds and $\neg P(h_2)$ holds, then no element of $\mathrm{Warrants}(o, y, P)$ can exist. In `chem/basin-compose.cub`, such a pair is formalized as a **`Disagreement` split witness**, and `disagreement-refutes-warrant` proves constructively that any split witness refutes warrant:

$$\mathrm{Disagreement}(o, y, P) \;\to\; \neg\,\mathrm{Warrants}(o, y, P)$$

This single type turns Karl Popper and David Deutsch's epistemology (*"a good explanation is hard to vary"*) into a machine-checked boundary condition: a conclusion is admissible if and only if **no history left open by the evidence can vary it**. Hallucination — asserting $P$ when the fiber still contains $\neg P$ — is structurally ill-typed.

### 1.3 Monotonicity of Refinement

Suppose a participant gains an additional observation, refining $o : H \to Y$ to $o' : H \to Y'$ with a projection $p : Y' \to Y$ satisfying $p(o'(h)) \equiv o(h)$. Every history compatible with the sharper reading $y'$ is automatically compatible with the coarser reading $p(y')$, giving a canonical fiber inclusion $\mathrm{Fib}_{o'}(y') \to \mathrm{Fib}_o(p(y'))$.

By precomposition (`warrants-refine` in `lib/observation.cub`), **every conclusion warranted by a coarser observation remains warranted under any refinement**, while strictly more conclusions become warranted as incompatible histories are ruled out. Conversely, if an action is currently unwarranted at $y$, active sensing means choosing a probe whose response splits $\mathrm{Fib}_o(y)$ until the resulting sub-fiber warrants a safe action.

